{
 "metadata": {
  "note": "Verbatim DDInter 2.0 slice for the load-time validity check on issue #476 - real shipped rows, unedited. ONE POSITIVE: Sulfamethoxazole (DDInter1724) publishes rxnorm_name `sulfamethazine` and sulfamethazine's CIEL concepts, while the dataset's own bridge files the concepts naming `sulfamethoxazole` as an ingredient (Sulfamethoxazole / trimethoprim, Phenazopyridine / sulfamethoxazole, Phenazopyridine / sulfamethoxazole / trimethoprim) on Trimethoprim and Phenazopyridine and not on it. CONTROLS that must NOT be reported: Sulfadiazine shares a concept with the positive and publishes its own name as rxnorm_name; Isosorbide is named as an ingredient of Hydralazine / isosorbide, filed on Hydralazine and not on it, but publishes its own name as rxnorm_name (the name-divergence half is load-bearing); Licorice is named as an ingredient of Benzocaine / licorice / menthol, filed on Benzocaine (topical) and not on it, but its rxnorm_name `licorice allergenic extract` carries its name as a word (a preparation, not another substance); Acetylsalicylic acid diverges from its rxnorm_name `aspirin` but the bridge never names `acetylsalicylic acid`, so nothing contradicts the synonym; Azelaic acid and Azelaic acid (topical) diverge from `azelate` and are named by a concept another row carries, but each carries that concept itself (the not-filed-on-this-row half is load-bearing)."
 },
 "mechanisms": {
  "57": {
   "text": "Licorice use has been associated with hypertension and may antagonize the effects of antihypertensive agents. Glycyrrhizic acid, a component of licorice, is hydrolyzed in the intestine to a metabolite (glycyrrhetinic acid) that causes mineralocorticoid and renin-suppressing effects.",
   "categories": [
    "antagonistic_effect"
   ]
  },
  "3020": {
   "text": "Some topical anesthetics can be systemically absorbed and cause methemoglobinemia, particularly when applied to mucous membranes. Coadministration with other oxidizing agents that can also induce methemoglobinemia such as injectable local anesthetics, antimalarials (e.g., chloroquine, primaquine, quinine, tafenoquine), nitrates and nitrites, sulfonamides, acetaminophen, aminosalicylic acid, dapsone, dimethyl sulfoxide, flutamide, metoclopramide, nitrofurantoin, phenazopyridine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, and rasburicase may increase the risk. Additional risk factors include very young age (e.g., infants less than 6 months); application to inflamed/abraded areas or broken skin; anemia; cardiac or pulmonary disease; peripheral vascular disease; liver cirrhosis; shock; sepsis; acidosis; and genetic predisposition (e.g., NADH cytochrome-b5 reductase deficiency; glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency; hemoglobin M).",
   "categories": [
    "synergistic_effect"
   ]
  },
  "7960": {
   "text": "Ester-type local anesthetics are hydrolyzed to paraaminobenzoic acid, which can inhibit the action of sulfonamides.",
   "categories": [
    "antagonistic_effect"
   ]
  },
  "-1": {
   "text": null,
   "categories": []
  },
  "4222": {
   "text": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) may attenuate the antihypertensive effect of hydralazine and dihydralazine. This interaction suggests that prostaglandins play a pivotal role in the vasodilatory effects of hydralazine.",
   "categories": [
    "antagonistic_effect"
   ]
  }
 },
